      <h2 id="why-different">Why website image compression is different</h2>
      <p>Website image compression isn't about email limits or form requirements. It's about speed, and speed affects your bottom line.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Pages that load in 1 second have roughly <strong>7x higher conversion rates</strong> than pages that load in 10 seconds (Google).</li>
        <li>Every 100ms of load delay costs about <strong>1% of users</strong>, who abandon the page (Amazon).</li>
        <li>Images typically account for <strong>50-80% of page weight</strong> — the rest is CSS, JavaScript, and other assets.</li>
        <li>Compressing images is the fastest, easiest way to improve page speed without rebuilding your site.</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="callout">
        <p><strong>For e-commerce:</strong> a 1-second faster page translates to roughly 10% higher conversion on average. For a site doing $100K/month, that's $10K in extra revenue per month from faster images alone.</p>
        <p><strong>For blogs and content sites:</strong> faster pages rank higher in Google search, since page speed is a ranking factor — and images are usually the bottleneck.</p>
        <p><strong>For social media (LinkedIn, Twitter, and similar):</strong> compressed images load instantly in feeds, which means more clicks and higher engagement.</p>
      </div>

      <h2 id="how-to-optimize">How to optimize images for web</h2>

      <h3>1. Compress for device type</h3>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Device</th>
            <th>Target size</th>
            <th>Why</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Desktop (1920&times;1080)</td>
            <td>200-300KB</td>
            <td>Fast WiFi, big screens, more patience — full-width hero images can afford more weight.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Mobile (375&times;812)</td>
            <td>50-100KB</td>
            <td>Often on 4G with a small screen — a 2MB photo buys nothing a 150KB one doesn't already show.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <p>Practical approach: <a href="/compress/to-100kb/">compress your image to 100KB</a>, upload two versions to your website, and use HTML <code>srcset</code> to serve the right one per screen size:</p>
      <pre><code class="language-html">&lt;img
  src="photo-desktop.jpg"
  srcset="photo-mobile.jpg 480w, photo-desktop.jpg 1200w"
  alt="Product photo"
&gt;</code></pre>
      <p>Result: mobile users get files roughly 50% smaller (faster load), while desktop users still get full quality.</p>

      <h3>2. Choose the right format</h3>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Format</th>
            <th>Best for</th>
            <th>Target size</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>JPG</td>
            <td>Product photos, hero images, blog photos — universal support, best quality-to-size ratio</td>
            <td>100-200KB</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>PNG</td>
            <td>Icons, logos, screenshots — lossless, supports transparency, but larger files</td>
            <td>50-150KB</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>WebP</td>
            <td>Modern sites — 25-30% smaller than JPG at the same quality, needs a JPG fallback for old browsers</td>
            <td>70-150KB</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>Recommendation: use JPG for e-commerce, blogs, and most websites. If your audience skews tech-forward, experiment with WebP.</p>

      <h3>3. Optimize for Core Web Vitals</h3>
      <p>Google partly ranks pages on Core Web Vitals — three metrics that measure real-world speed:</p>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Metric</th>
            <th>Measures</th>
            <th>Target</th>
            <th>How compression helps</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>LCP <span style="color: var(--text-secondary); font-weight: 400;">(Largest Contentful Paint)</span></td>
            <td>How fast the main image or content appears</td>
            <td>Under 2.5s</td>
            <td><a href="/compress/to-200kb/">Compress hero images to 150-200KB</a> to hit this directly.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>CLS <span style="color: var(--text-secondary); font-weight: 400;">(Cumulative Layout Shift)</span></td>
            <td>How much content shifts as images load</td>
            <td>Under 0.1</td>
            <td>Faster loads shorten the window content can shift in. Also set explicit width/height on <code>&lt;img&gt;</code> tags.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>FID <span style="color: var(--text-secondary); font-weight: 400;">(First Input Delay)</span></td>
            <td>How fast the page responds to clicks</td>
            <td>Mostly JS-driven</td>
            <td>Smaller images mean less decode/CPU work, indirectly helping response time.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>Practical step: <a href="/compress/to-200kb/">compress your product and hero images to under 200KB</a>. This alone improves LCP significantly on most sites.</p>

      <h3>4. Batch compress if you have many images</h3>
      <p>If your site has 50+ product photos or blog images, don't compress them one by one. <a href="/compress/to-100kb/">Compress one image with Filekrush</a> as a template, note the compression ratio (for example, 2MB to 100KB is 95% compression), then apply the same ratio to the rest using a batch tool like ImageMagick, Photoshop, or your CMS's image optimization plugin. Verify the final result loads fast.</p>

      <h2 id="example">Real-world example: e-commerce product page</h2>
